cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: >-
  rareverify: planning and executing a Monte-Carlo verification campaign for a
  rare requirement violation, with defensible interval bounds
version: 0.1.0
date-released: 2026-10-08
license: Apache-2.0
abstract: >-
  A rare-event verification campaign, treated end to end: how many runs the
  campaign needs, how those runs should be spent, and which interval may be
  quoted afterwards. Sample-size planning separates demonstration from
  estimation and gives the exact closed form for the zero-failure case a real
  campaign normally reaches. Clopper-Pearson and Wilson intervals are provided
  with their coverage computed exactly by summation over the binomial mass
  rather than by simulation, which shows the Wilson interval under-covering to
  0.9046 against a nominal 0.95 and shows the usual ordering of the two
  intervals reversing at k = 0 below n = 46. Variance reduction is offered in
  one explicitly declared family -- the mean-shift family on standard normal
  space, which for the Gaussian is exponential tilting -- and in subset
  simulation, and every reduction factor reported is an empirical variance over
  independent replications on an equal true-evaluation basis, not a formula
  prediction. The cases where importance sampling is WORSE than no importance
  sampling are a deliverable rather than an omission: 26 of 40 swept tilts are
  measurably worse than plain Monte Carlo, one of them returning an estimate
  2.07e5 times too small while its variance reduction factor reads 1.8e+08,
  which is why a mean-squared-error reduction factor is reported beside it. A
  Gaussian-process surrogate of the limit-state function supplies the mean
  shift when no analytic design point exists; it is benchmarked against the
  analytic baseline, which was implemented first, on an equal budget of true
  limit-state evaluations with its training set counted against it. It has
  nothing to win on a smooth analytic limit state, where it recovers the
  analytic design point to 5.890e-06 and pays its training cost for nothing,
  and it wins by a measured factor of 10.66 on a rough one while falling 31 per
  cent short of an oracle tilt. Both outcomes are published. Every probability
  the package reports is the violation probability of the model that was
  simulated, not of any vehicle. Validation level 2, research grade. Not
  flight-qualified, not certified, not approved for operational aerospace use.
authors:
  - name: "OPTIMA Organisation"
keywords:
  - rare-event-simulation
  - monte-carlo
  - importance-sampling
  - exponential-tilting
  - subset-simulation
  - clopper-pearson
  - wilson-interval
  - reliability-analysis
  - verification-campaign
  - gaussian-process-surrogate
references:
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Clopper
        given-names: "C. J."
      - family-names: Pearson
        given-names: "E. S."
    title: >-
      The use of confidence or fiducial limits illustrated in the case of the
      binomial
    journal: "Biometrika"
    volume: 26
    issue: 4
    year: 1934
    notes: >-
      The exact interval implemented in rareverify.intervals.clopper_pearson
      and the source of the zero-failure closed form the planner inverts.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Wilson
        given-names: "E. B."
    title: >-
      Probable inference, the law of succession, and statistical inference
    journal: "Journal of the American Statistical Association"
    volume: 22
    issue: 158
    year: 1927
    notes: >-
      The score interval implemented in rareverify.intervals.wilson. Its exact
      coverage is computed in validation/validate_intervals.py and dips to
      0.9046 against a nominal 0.95 on the tested grid.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Brown
        given-names: "L. D."
      - family-names: Cai
        given-names: "T. T."
      - family-names: DasGupta
        given-names: "A."
    title: "Interval estimation for a binomial proportion"
    journal: "Statistical Science"
    volume: 16
    issue: 2
    year: 2001
    notes: >-
      The recommendation of the score interval over the Wald interval, and the
      analysis of the oscillating coverage this package measures directly.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Agresti
        given-names: "A."
      - family-names: Coull
        given-names: "B. A."
    title: >-
      Approximate is better than 'exact' for interval estimation of binomial
      proportions
    journal: "The American Statistician"
    volume: 52
    issue: 2
    year: 1998
    notes: >-
      The case against the exact interval on grounds of length, which this
      package quotes alongside its own coverage measurements rather than
      choosing a side.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Hanley
        given-names: "J. A."
      - family-names: Lippman-Hand
        given-names: "A."
    title: >-
      If nothing goes wrong, is everything all right? Interpreting zero
      numerators
    journal: "JAMA"
    volume: 249
    issue: 13
    year: 1983
    notes: >-
      The rule of three, implemented as rule_of_three_upper so that its
      relative error can be measured; it tends to 1.4246021e-3, not to zero.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Au
        given-names: "S.-K."
      - family-names: Beck
        given-names: "J. L."
    title: >-
